WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:05.231
Dev is where new versions land first.

2
00:00:05.231 --> 00:00:06.784
Prod is what users see.

3
00:00:06.784 --> 00:00:10.497
Here both live in your kind cluster, in two namespaces.

4
00:00:10.667 --> 00:00:12.396
Dev runs one Pod.

5
00:00:12.396 --> 00:00:13.923
Prod runs four.

6
00:00:13.923 --> 00:00:16.873
Both run version 1.0.0 today.

7
00:00:17.067 --> 00:00:20.931
In git, each environment has its own Kustomize overlay.

8
00:00:20.931 --> 00:00:23.672
The line that matters is the image tag.

9
00:00:23.867 --> 00:00:27.029
Argo CD needs one Application per environment.

10
00:00:27.029 --> 00:00:34.248
An ApplicationSet writes them from one template: a list of values, and an Application with a placeholder.

11
00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:37.168
The controller fills it in once per entry.

12
00:00:37.168 --> 00:00:40.728
Two Applications appear, one for dev and one for prod.

13
00:00:40.867 --> 00:00:45.673
Each one watches its own folder in git, and deploys it to its own namespace.

14
00:00:45.867 --> 00:00:48.551
Now you have a new version, 1.1.0.

15
00:00:48.551 --> 00:00:52.024
How does it travel from dev to prod, safely?

16
00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:53.169
Step one.

17
00:00:53.169 --> 00:00:58.444
You push the tag v1.1.0, and CI builds the image.

18
00:00:58.444 --> 00:00:58.983
Once.

19
00:00:59.133 --> 00:00:59.823
Step two.

20
00:00:59.823 --> 00:01:03.733
A pull request changes one line in the dev overlay.

21
00:01:03.933 --> 00:01:05.263
You merge it.

22
00:01:05.263 --> 00:01:08.128
Now git says dev runs 1.1.0.

23
00:01:08.267 --> 00:01:12.305
Argo CD sees the change in its folder, and deploys dev.

24
00:01:12.467 --> 00:01:13.627
Step three.

25
00:01:13.627 --> 00:01:14.997
You test dev.

26
00:01:15.133 --> 00:01:16.036
Step four.

27
00:01:16.036 --> 00:01:21.632
A second pull request sets the prod overlay to the same 1.1.0.

28
00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:24.004
Someone reviews it, and merges it.

29
00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:29.771
Argo CD syncs prod, and the rollout replaces the four prod Pods, one at a time.

30
00:01:29.771 --> 00:01:33.579
Dev is not touched, because its folder did not change.

31
00:01:34.267 --> 00:01:36.220
Notice what never happened.

32
00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:38.247
The image was never rebuilt.

33
00:01:38.247 --> 00:01:41.069
Prod runs the exact bytes that dev ran.

34
00:01:41.069 --> 00:01:42.805
Build once, deploy many.

35
00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:44.903
Who writes that line?

36
00:01:44.903 --> 00:01:52.416
The classic tool, kustomize edit set image, rewrote the whole prod file: 80 lines changed to set one tag.

37
00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:55.290
yq changes only the field you name.

38
00:01:55.290 --> 00:01:55.982
One line.

39
00:01:55.982 --> 00:02:02.668
The lab's bump-image workflow uses yq, then pushes a branch and opens the pull request.

40
00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:04.957
And if prod misbehaves?

41
00:02:04.957 --> 00:02:07.666
Press Revert on the merged pull request.

42
00:02:07.666 --> 00:02:10.780
It opens a new one that puts the old tag back.

43
00:02:10.933 --> 00:02:15.185
Merge it, and Argo CD puts 1.0.0 back.

44
00:02:15.733 --> 00:02:19.184
This only works if a tag always means the same image.

45
00:02:19.184 --> 00:02:21.528
Say the overlay said latest instead.

46
00:02:21.667 --> 00:02:24.865
latest points at different images over time.

47
00:02:24.865 --> 00:02:25.883
Today image A.

48
00:02:26.067 --> 00:02:28.009
Tomorrow it moves to image B.

49
00:02:28.009 --> 00:02:30.354
But the file in git did not change.

50
00:02:30.354 --> 00:02:35.111
So Argo CD sees nothing to sync, and git no longer tells you what runs.

51
00:02:35.267 --> 00:02:38.907
And a revert cannot bring the old version back.

52
00:02:38.907 --> 00:02:43.941
Use a version tag like 1.1.0, or a digest, which nobody can move.

53
00:02:44.133 --> 00:02:45.628
Build the image once.

54
00:02:45.628 --> 00:02:50.110
Then promote the same tag from dev to prod with a pull request.

55
00:02:50.110 --> 00:02:53.455
Review it, merge it, and let Argo CD deploy it.
